w-up (with optional contrast). This distinction is important for clinical decision-making when cumulative radiation exposure or modality appropriateness is a concern. [Data: Entities (1868, 671)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Risk management includes explicit disclosure and mitigation steps for contrast-related allergic reactions\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Allergic reaction is represented as a potential adverse event in clinical settings, particularly in connection with contrast material used in imaging. The relationships specify that patients are instructed to tell their doctor about prior problems with contrast, signaling a risk-screening step that can alter how imaging is performed (e.g., premedication strategies or contrast avoidance, as described in the entity definition). [Data: Entities (2075, 25); Relationships (9348)]\\n\\nThis contrasts-related safety thread complements other pre-scan disclosures (such as metal for MRI) and reinforces that the doctor serves as the central risk triage point for determining which tests are safe and appropriate for a given patient. The overall network therefore encodes a procedural compliance pattern: disclose risks, route them to the clinician, and adapt testing accordingly. [Data: Entities (25, 1540, 2075); Relationships (3212, 9348)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Care teams use imaging to evaluate suspected cancer growth and escalate to additional imaging when indicated\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The care team is explicitly linked to recommending imaging tests to look for cancer growth, establishing imaging as a monitoring tool used when progression is a concern. This indicates a surveillance/assessment loop in which imaging is used to answer a concrete clinical question (growth/progression) rather than being purely diagnostic at baseline. [Data: Entities (3266); Relationships (6437)]\\n\\nA related operational step is the care team requesting more imaging when there may be cancer, which reflects escalation logic: initial images or clinical suspicion can trigger additional scans to clarify findings, extent, or next actions. Together, these relationships describe an iterative diagnostic process rather than a single test event. [Data: Entities (1043); Relationships (1527)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Staging and scoring are formal decision pivots that connect test results to treatment selection\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Cancer stage is defined as a standardized descriptor of disease extent, with staging determined by integrating imaging and other diagnostic information. The relationships directly connect cancer stage to treatment, stating that cancer treatment is based on stage—encoding staging as a primary decision driver in this community. [Data: Entities (510); Relationships (2272)]\\n\\nThe doctor’s role in operationalizing staging is also explicit: cancer test results are used by the doctor to assign TNM scores, and the doctor assigns letter-number scores to T, N, and M. This links the informational output of testing (including imaging) to structured classification used for planning and eligibility decisions. [Data: Entities (3900, 3901, 25); Relationships (8164, 8165)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"PET-style interpretation is represented via ‘bright spots’ and the metabolic basis of tracer uptake—along with non-specificity caveats\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The network includes a diagnostic-imaging interpretation concept where bright spots on PET (and related nuclear medicine studies) correspond to areas of increased radiotracer uptake and may align with cancer cell activity. This is tied mechanistically to cancer cells using sugar more rapidly than many normal tissues, which provides the metabolic basis for tracer accumulation patterns. [Data: Entities (1570, 310, 323); Relationships (2589, 417)]\\n\\nHowever, the bright-spots entity definition also emphasizes interpretive caution: bright spots are not specific to cancer and can reflect normal physiologic uptake or benign processes such as inflammation or infection. In community terms, this means imaging signals require clinical context and professional interpretation rather than being definitive on their own. [Data: Entities (1570)]\\n\\nThe inclusion of glucose/sugar phrasing tied to cancer cells reinforces the same concept: increased sugar use by cancer cells can drive brighter PET appearance, connecting biological behavior to imaging findings. [Data: Entities (6081, 310); Relationships (13900)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Treatment pathways are represented through chemotherapy delivery mechanics and radiation therapy planning constraints\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Chemotherapy administration is linked to intravenous injection as the common delivery mechanism, establishing an operational treatment capability and an associated clinical procedure (IV administration) within the community. This reflects that treatment logistics (not just drug choice) are part of the care network. [Data: Entities (1938); Relationships (3256)]\\n\\nRadiation is represented both as a physical phenomenon used in medicine and as a treatment modality via radiation therapy. The community also includes a customized radiation plan tailored to the patient’s body and cancer, indicating individualized planning as a key technical step in delivering radiation therapy. [Data: Entities (671, 5074); Relationships (930, 11144)]\\n\\nTreatment constraints also appear: in local or regional recurrence, prior radiation exposure can limit whether radiation therapy remains an option. This embeds a longitudinal dependency (past treatment affects future options) that can materially affect decision-making. [Data: Entities (4889); Relationships (10766)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Role differentiation and communication norms: radiologists interpret imaging; doctors integrate results and guide patient communication/quality checks\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The rad